NNAAMMEE
       pam_exec - PAM module which calls an external command

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppaamm__eexxeecc..ssoo [debug] [expose_authtok] [seteuid] [quiet] [quiet_log]
                   [stdout] [log=_f_i_l_e] [type=_t_y_p_e] _c_o_m_m_a_n_d [_._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       pam_exec is a PAM module that can be used to run an external command.

       The child's environment is set to the current PAM environment list, as
       returned by ppaamm__ggeetteennvvlliisstt(3) In addition, the following PAM items are
       exported as environment variables: _P_A_M___R_H_O_S_T, _P_A_M___R_U_S_E_R, _P_A_M___S_E_R_V_I_C_E,
       _P_A_M___T_T_Y, _P_A_M___U_S_E_R and _P_A_M___T_Y_P_E, which contains one of the module types:
       aaccccoouunntt, aauutthh, ppaasssswwoorrdd, ooppeenn__sseessssiioonn and cclloossee__sseessssiioonn.

       Commands called by pam_exec need to be aware of that the user can have
       control over the environment.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       ddeebbuugg
           Print debug information.

       eexxppoossee__aauutthhttookk
           During authentication the calling command can read the password
           from ssttddiinn(3). Only first _P_A_M___M_A_X___R_E_S_P___S_I_Z_E bytes of a password are
           provided to the command.

       lloogg==_f_i_l_e
           The output of the command is appended to file

       ttyyppee==_t_y_p_e
           Only run the command if the module type matches the given type.

       ssttddoouutt
           Per default the output of the executed command is written to
           /dev/null. With this option, the stdout output of the executed
           command is redirected to the calling application. It's in the
           responsibility of this application what happens with the output.
           The lloogg option is ignored.

       qquuiieett
           Per default pam_exec.so will echo the exit status of the external
           command if it fails. Specifying this option will suppress the
           message.

       qquuiieett__lloogg
           Per default pam_exec.so will log the exit status of the external
           command if it fails. Specifying this option will suppress the log
           message.

       sseetteeuuiidd
           Per default pam_exec.so will execute the external command with the
           real user ID of the calling process. Specifying this option means
           the command is run with the effective user ID.

MMOODDUULLEE TTYYPPEESS PPRROOVVIIDDEEDD
       All module types (aauutthh, aaccccoouunntt, ppaasssswwoorrdd and sseessssiioonn) are provided.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
       PAM_SUCCESS
           The external command was run successfully.

       PAM_BUF_ERR
           Memory buffer error.

       PAM_CONV_ERR
           The conversation method supplied by the application failed to
           obtain the username.

       PAM_INCOMPLETE
           The conversation method supplied by the application returned
           PAM_CONV_AGAIN.

       PAM_SERVICE_ERR
           No argument or a wrong number of arguments were given.

       PAM_SYSTEM_ERR
           A system error occurred or the command to execute failed.

       PAM_IGNORE
           ppaamm__sseettccrreedd was called, which does not execute the command. Or, the
           value given for the type= parameter did not match the module type.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       Add the following line to /etc/pam.d/passwd to rebuild the NIS database
       after each local password change:

                   password optional pam_exec.so seteuid /usr/bin/make -C /var/yp


       This will execute the command

           make -C /var/yp

       with effective user ID.

